Q: Javascript bundled with webpack not working A: <p>This is expected behavior with webpack. When you load <code>app.js</code> directly in HTML, everything in that file ends up in the global scope. So functions are available on <code>window</code>, inline handlers work, and calling them from the console works.</p>
<p>When you load <code>bundle.js</code>, webpack wraps every file in its own function. Your <code>app.js</code> code is no longer global. The functions exist, but only inside the module wrapper, so anything outside the bundle sees them as <code>undefined</code>. That’s why copying the function to the top of <code>bundle.js</code> “fixes” it, because you’re accidentally putting it back in global scope.</p>
<p>If your HTML uses things like <code>onclick=&quot;myFunction()&quot;</code>, they will break once you bundle unless you explicitly expose the function. The simplest fix is to attach the functions you need to <code>window</code>:</p>
<pre><code>function myFunction() {
  // ...
}

window.myFunction = myFunction;
</code></pre>
<p>That makes it behave the same way as when you load <code>app.js</code> directly.</p>
<p>A cleaner fix is to stop using inline handlers entirely and wire everything up in JS (query the element and addEventListener), but that’s a bigger refactor. Also, your HTML is still loading <code>./scripts/app.js</code>. When you bundle, you should be loading <code>./scripts/bundle.js</code> instead, otherwise you’re mixing source files with bundled output.</p>
<p>So the short version is that webpack is doing exactly what it’s supposed to do. Your functions aren’t global anymore, and you either need to expose them or stop relying on globals.</p>
